(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-02"] = {"4.1":{"s":"Atoms are made of three subatomic particles. The electron, discovered by J. J. Thomson, is negatively charged and very light. The proton, identified by Rutherford, is positively charged and about 1800 times heavier than the electron. The neutron, discovered by Chadwick, has no charge and nearly the same mass as the proton. Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus; electrons move around it.","w":["The neutron is neutral, so it is the only one of the three that a magnetic or electric field cannot deflect."]},"4.2":{"s":"The atomic number Z is the number of protons in the nucleus; it is also the number of electrons in a neutral atom, and it identifies the element. The mass number A is the number of protons plus neutrons, the number of nucleons. The number of neutrons is therefore A minus Z. An atom is written with A as a superscript and Z as a subscript before the symbol.","f":[{"x":"N = A - Z","n":"Number of neutrons N from the mass number A and the atomic number Z.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.2, p.36"}],"w":["A cation has fewer electrons than protons and an anion more, but Z does not change."]},"4.3":{"s":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. Isobars have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Isotones have the same number of neutrons but different atomic and mass numbers. Isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties but slightly different physical properties.","w":["Isotopes: same Z. Isobars: same A. Isotones: same number of neutrons."]},"4.4":{"s":"Rutherford's model has a small, heavy, positive nucleus with electrons moving round it. Its first drawback: by Maxwell's theory, a charged particle in orbit is accelerated and must radiate energy, so the electron would spiral into the nucleus and the atom would collapse. The second: the model does not describe how the electrons are arranged or what their energies are."},"4.5":{"s":"Two developments led to Bohr's model. Light behaves as a wave (with wavelength, frequency and speed) and also as a stream of photons, each with energy proportional to its frequency. Hydrogen gas heated or excited gives a line emission spectrum, not a continuous one. The lines fall in series: Lyman (ultraviolet), Balmer (visible), Paschen, Brackett and Pfund (infrared). Balmer and Rydberg found a formula for their wavenumbers.","f":[{"x":"E = h*nu = h*c/lambda","n":"Energy of a photon: frequency nu, wavelength lambda, speed of light c, Planck's constant h.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.5, p.39"},{"x":"nu_bar = R_H*(1/n_1^2 - 1/n_2^2)","n":"Rydberg formula for the wavenumber of the hydrogen lines; R_H = 109677 per cm; n_2 > n_1. The Balmer series has n_1 = 2.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.5, p.40"}],"w":["Shorter wavelength means higher frequency and higher energy: blue light carries more energy per photon than red.","Series: Lyman n₁ = 1 (UV), Balmer 2 (visible), Paschen 3, Brackett 4, Pfund 5 (all infrared)."]},"4.6":{"s":"Bohr's model for hydrogen has electrons moving only in certain circular orbits, the stationary states, without radiating. Energy is emitted or absorbed only when an electron jumps between orbits, and the photon energy equals the difference. The energy and radius depend on the principal quantum number n. The energy becomes less negative as n rises, and the radius grows as n². It works for one-electron species such as H, He⁺ and Li²⁺ only.","f":[{"x":"E_n = -2.18*10^-18*(Z^2/n^2) J","n":"Energy of the electron in the n-th orbit of a hydrogen-like species of atomic number Z.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.6, p.42"},{"x":"r_n = 52.9*n^2/Z pm","n":"Radius of the n-th orbit.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.6, p.42"},{"x":"Delta E = E_f - E_i = R_H*(1/n_i^2 - 1/n_f^2)","n":"Energy change when the electron jumps from orbit n_i to n_f.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.6, p.42"}],"w":["Bohr's model works only for one-electron species: H, He⁺, Li²⁺.","Energy is negative and rises towards zero as n increases; n = 1 is the most stable level."]},"4.7":{"s":"Bohr's model failed for bigger atoms, and it ignored two ideas: de Broglie's dual nature of matter and Heisenberg's uncertainty principle. De Broglie said that a particle has a wavelength. Heisenberg said we cannot know exactly both the position and the momentum of an electron at the same time. Quantum mechanics replaced orbits by orbitals, regions of high probability, found from the Schrödinger equation. Electrons fill orbitals by the Aufbau, Pauli and Hund rules.","f":[{"x":"lambda = h/(m*v) = h/p","n":"de Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass m and speed v.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.7, p.44"},{"x":"Delta x * Delta p >= h/(4*pi)","n":"Heisenberg uncertainty principle: position and momentum cannot both be known exactly.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.7, p.44"}],"w":["An orbital is a region of probability, not a path; the Bohr orbit and the orbital are different things.","Pauli: no two electrons in an atom have the same four quantum numbers (an orbital holds at most two, with opposite spins). Hund: fill degenerate orbitals singly first."]}};
